A68. An unfair place to get a "square" meal. ~ RESTAURANT

D1. The shorter a Scot wears 'em, the longer he wears 'em. ~ PLAIDS
D2. Substantial Swedish cognomen, for females. ~ HILDA
D3. How a vice president of a bank feels. ~ IDLE
D4. Belonging to. ~ OF
D5. State of affairs critics observe at funny shows. ~ SAD
D6. A news notice everybody gets—too late. ~ OBIT
D7. What bootleggers should be given a chance to do in court. ~ PLEAD
D8. A good companion for She. ~ HE
D9. You identify them by the pants. ~ MALES
D10. A crackshot—he cracks a heart, every shot. ~ EROS
D11. Picture of a bygone being a bygone. ~ AGO
D12. A full house. Poker flats, as Bret Harte said. ~ TENEMENT
D15. Nice over here, but plain crazy in England. ~ BALMY
D17. The contest all night students entered. ~ YAWNING
D19. He delivers baby all right but presents a bill first. ~ STORK
D21. A head burn. ~ SINGE
D23. The sergeant who was bad in language. ~ TOP
D26. This gets floored in all arenas and doesn't get up. ~ GUM
D28. Bird with a little call. ~ PEWEE
D29. The practical way to feel sorry. ~ ATONE
D30. A seat for every child—if it's a co-ed. ~ LAP
D32. The kind of meals elephants prefer. ~ LARGE
D33. What standing opera patrons do after they get tired. ~ STAND
D34. This will give you your last slap in the face. ~ SOD
